    description: "Responsible charge on design-build sits with the licensed PE who directs the engineering. How firms assign it, document it, and keep every seal legitimate.",
    h1: "Who Holds Responsible Charge on a Design-Build Project?",
    answer: "On a design-build project, responsible charge sits with the licensed professional engineer who actually directs and controls the engineering work — the PE who makes the engineering decisions, supervises the designers, reviews the calculations, and ultimately seals the drawings. It does not sit with the design-build firm as an entity, with the project manager, with the unlicensed designer who drafted the sheets, or with a PE elsewhere in the company who never touched the project. Every state board — TBPELS in Texas through Rule 137.33, the Arizona Board of Technical Registration, California, Nevada, and Florida's boards — defines it the same essential way: the sealing engineer must have performed the work or exercised direct supervision and control over it.\n\nI see design-build firms get this wrong in two directions. The first is diffusion: so many hands touch the engineering — the firm's designers, the MEP subs, the steel fabricator's detailer — that nobody can say who was in responsible charge of what, and the seals at the end are applied by whoever is available. The second is concentration: one PE's name goes on everything while the actual engineering was done by unlicensed staff the PE never supervised. Both are board violations, and both surface at the worst times — during a plan-check challenge, a dispute, or a board complaint. The fix is a written responsible-charge map: every engineered scope traced to a named PE who genuinely directed it.\n\nDocumenting responsible charge is a project management discipline, not just an ethical one. I advise design-build firms to record it the way they record everything else: the PE's review comments at each milestone, the calculation verification, the revision directives, the re-review of corrections. That record is what makes the seal defensible — it is the evidence that responsible charge was real. When the AHJ questions a seal, when an owner disputes the engineering, when a board asks, the firm answers with the record. Firms that document responsible charge as they go never scramble to reconstruct it later.",
