    h1: "How Is MEP Engineering Sealed on Design-Build Projects?",
    answer: "MEP engineering on design-build is sealed by the licensed PE in responsible charge of the MEP design — either the design-build firm's own MEP engineer or the design-build MEP subcontractor's engineer — after a genuine review: verified load calculations, checked equipment selections, code-compliant distribution and plumbing design, coordinated drawings, and required revisions made. The design-build wrinkle is that MEP design often develops inside the subcontractor's shop — the mechanical sub designing the HVAC, the electrical sub laying out distribution — which means the responsible PE has to be embedded in that process, reviewing as the design develops, not handed a finished package at permit time. A seal applied to MEP drawings the PE never reviewed is not a design-build efficiency; it is a board violation.\n\nI see two healthy models. In the first, the design-build firm employs its own MEP EOR who designs (or directly supervises the design of) the MEP systems and seals them — clean responsible charge, continuous coordination with the firm's structural and architectural work. In the second, the MEP subs carry the design under their own licensed PEs, and the firm's MEP coordinator (or an outside reviewing PE) performs conformance review against the performance criteria. Both work; what does not work is the gap between them — the project where the subs \"engineered\" the systems, nobody's PE reviewed them, and the permit set arrives at the AHJ with MEP sheets that have no seal at all. I map every MEP scope to its sealing PE at project setup, before the subs start designing.\n\nThe MEP review substance does not change for design-build: HVAC loads verified against real design conditions, equipment matched to the loads including latent capacity in humid climates, plumbing risers and fixture counts checked, electrical distribution coordinated from service to branch circuits, lighting and controls compliant, and energy documentation reconciled to the sealed design — Title 24 in California, the applicable energy code in the other states. Design-build's speed comes from overlapping design and construction, and the MEP seal program has to be sequenced for that: phased MEP submittals (underground and rough-in packages sealed early, finish packages later) keep construction moving while the review stays genuine. Our /pe-stamp/ practice seals MEP for design-build teams in all five states — the review is the same rigor, sequenced for the delivery method.",
    directAnswer: "MEP on design-build is sealed by the licensed PE in responsible charge of the MEP design — the firm's own MEP engineer or the sub's PE — after verifying loads, equipment, distribution, and energy compliance. Map every MEP scope to its sealing PE at setup, and sequence phased MEP submittals to the fast-track schedule.",
