    answer: "Design-build teams coordinate permits with sealed drawings by phasing both: the PE's review-and-seal sequence and the AHJ's submittal sequence are planned together, matched to the construction schedule, so each phase's sealed package arrives when the field needs it. The classic structure is foundations and site work first — reviewed, sealed, permitted while the superstructure is still in design — then the full building permit, with MEP and delegated components following as phased or deferred submittals per the AHJ's policy. None of this works as improvisation; it works as a permit plan built at project setup, with the PE's review milestones, the AHJ's intake requirements, and the construction sequence on the same timeline.\n\nI build the permit plan around three inputs. First, the AHJ's actual requirements: which phases they accept, what each phased submittal must contain, their digital seal acceptance, their review timelines, and their correction patterns — confirmed with the jurisdiction, not assumed from the last project in a different city. A phased strategy that works in Houston's ProjectDox may not map to Phoenix's electronic review or Miami-Dade's process. Second, the PE's review capacity: phased submittals multiply the review effort (each phase gets a genuine review and seal), so the engineering schedule has to carry it. Third, the construction sequence: the permit phases must release work in the order the field needs it, with float for at least one correction round per phase.\n\nThe coordination discipline that holds it together is the permit dashboard: every phased package tracked from PE review through AHJ approval, with owners, dates, and status visible to the whole team. The PE's review status, the submittal date, the reviewer's comments, the correction response, the re-seal, the resubmittal — each phase moves through the same pipeline, and the dashboard shows where every package sits. Design-build teams that run this visibly keep permits ahead of construction; teams that manage permits by hallway conversation discover the missing seal the week the steel arrives. If your design-build team needs the engineering side of that coordination handled, our /pe-stamp/ practice runs phased review-and-seal programs for design-build firms across all five states.",
    directAnswer: "Design-build permit coordination means planning the PE's phased review-and-seal sequence and the AHJ's phased submittals together at project setup — foundations first, then building, then MEP/delegated packages — tracked on a visible permit dashboard with correction float built into every phase.",
