    title: "Do Owner-Builders Need a PE Stamp for Their Permit?",
    description: "Owner-builders need a PE stamp when the AHJ requires sealed engineering. When it triggers, what the review covers, and how to budget for it.",
    h1: "Do Owner-Builders Need a PE Stamp for Their Permit?",
    answer: "Owner-builders need a PE stamp whenever the building department requires sealed engineering for the project — acting as your own general contractor does not exempt you from the engineering requirements that apply to everyone else. The triggers are the same ones a GC faces: the project type or size crosses the state threshold for sealed design, the structure needs engineered foundations, framing, or lateral design the prescriptive code does not cover, or the AHJ requires sealed MEP, civil, or specialty engineering. In Florida the statutory thresholds are explicit and strictly applied; in California, seismic design requirements pull most structures beyond prescriptive construction into engineered design; in Arizona, Texas, and Nevada, the triggers vary by jurisdiction but the principle is identical: if the code requires engineering, it requires a licensed engineer's seal.\n\nI tell owner-builders to find out early, because the PE requirement changes the project budget and timeline in ways first-timers do not expect. The building department's intake checklist — available before you apply — states what sealed engineering the project needs; I recommend owner-builders confirm it in a pre-submittal visit or call rather than discovering it at the counter with a rejected application. The review-and-seal engagement itself is straightforward: a licensed PE in the project state reviews the design (yours, your designer's, or your architect's), verifies the calculations, requires revisions, and seals the permit set. The PE cannot seal what they have not reviewed — no legitimate engineer sells a signature — so the engagement takes the time a real review takes.\n\nThe owner-builder's real challenge is usually the design starting point. Many owner-builders arrive with partial plans — a floor plan sketched with a designer, a framing concept from a lumber yard, truss quotes with no engineering — and the PE's review has to start by establishing what the design actually is before it can verify it. I scope owner-builder engagements pragmatically: tell me what you have, I tell you what the review needs, and we fill the gaps in the cheapest order. Sometimes that means the PE does more design development than a pure \"review\" would suggest — which is fine, as long as the PE genuinely performs it under responsible charge. Budget for the engineering as a defined scope with a written agreement, the same as any other trade, and the permit process treats you like the professional you are acting as.",
