    h1: "What Does a Structural PE Review for an Owner-Builder?",
    answer: "A structural PE review for an owner-builder covers the complete structural design — foundations, gravity framing, and the lateral system — verifying each against the code, the soils report, and the laws of physics, in that order of consequence. I review the foundation design against the geotechnical recommendations first, because foundation errors are the most expensive to fix after construction starts. Then the gravity system: beams, joists, posts, and slabs checked against the actual loads, with the calculations verified independently rather than trusted. Then the lateral system: the shear walls or frames, the holdowns and connections, the drag struts — the complete load path from roof to foundation, detailed so it can actually be built. Only when all three check out, with required revisions made and re-reviewed, does the seal go on.\n\nI am direct with owner-builders about what the review usually finds. Owner-built structural designs — whether drawn by the owner, a designer, or a lumber-yard takeoff — most often fail in the same places: undersized beams over the big openings, missing or undersized holdowns at shear walls, drag struts nobody detailed, point loads landing on framing never designed for them, and foundations designed for soil conditions nobody verified. None of this is a criticism of the effort; it is simply what professional structural review exists to catch. The review's job is to find these on paper, where fixes cost revision clouds, instead of in the field, where they cost change orders — or after occupancy, where they cost far more.\n\nThe review also produces something owner-builders undervalue until inspection day: a buildable, coordinated structural set. I detail the connections, call out the holdown schedule, tie the foundation plan to the soils report, and make sure the framing plans agree with the architectural backgrounds — because the owner-builder is often also the framer reading these sheets at 6 a.m. A sealed structural set that the builder can actually build from is worth more than the permit it unlocks; it is the difference between a smooth frame and a summer of RFIs. That is the standard I seal to, and it is what the /pe-stamp/ review delivers for owner-builders.",
