        heading: "Foundations: where the review starts",
        body: "I start every owner-builder structural review at the ground, because everything above inherits the foundation's assumptions. The soils report gets read first — bearing values, expansive-soil potential, groundwater, the recommended foundation system — and the foundation design gets checked against it line by line: footing sizes against the bearing capacity, pier depths against the active zone, slab detailing against the expansiveness, drainage and moisture protection against the site's water behavior. The most common foundation finding on owner-builder reviews is a design that ignores the soils report entirely — a conventional slab drawn for soil the report describes as highly expansive.\n\nWhere no soils report exists, I determine whether the project can proceed on presumptive values or whether the investigation is non-negotiable — and I make that call conservatively, because the PE sealing the foundation owns the geotechnical assumption. I also review the foundation's constructability for the owner-builder specifically: formed footings versus trench footings, pier drilling access on the actual site, the drainage detailing the owner will actually install. A foundation design the owner cannot build correctly is not a good design, however elegant the calculations.",
