        heading: "The three letters owner-builders meet most",
        body: "First, the structural observation letter: the PE visits the site at the required stages (often before cover — framing, shear walls, holdowns, foundation steel), observes the work against the sealed drawings, and writes the sealed letter stating conformance or noting the deficiencies to correct. I coordinate observation visits with the owner-builder's inspection schedule so one trip serves both the observation and the inspection prep. The letter goes into the permit file and the inspector reads it — a clean observation letter smooths every subsequent inspection.\n\nSecond, the adequacy or evaluation letter for existing conditions: the owner-builder's project incorporates something already there — an existing foundation, a retaining wall, a structure being added to — and the AHJ wants an engineer's statement that it is adequate for the new loads. I investigate: review whatever documentation exists, visit and observe, perform the structural analysis, and write the letter with the basis, the calculations referenced, and the limitations stated plainly. Where the existing element cannot be verified without destructive investigation, I say so and recommend the investigation — a letter that overstates its basis is worse than no letter.\n\nThird, the special-inspection final letter: where the permit required special inspections (structural masonry, driven piles, spray-applied fireproofing, certain welding), the inspecting engineer's final sealed letter summarizes the inspections performed and states conformance with the approved documents. I run special-inspection programs with the reporting built in — each inspection documented as it happens — so the final letter writes itself from the record. Owner-builders sometimes discover the special-inspection requirement at the inspection stage; the time to engage the inspecting engineer is at permit issuance, when the inspection program gets defined.",
