    h1: "How Do PE Requirements Compare for Owner-Builders in Arizona, Texas, and Florida?",
    answer: "Arizona, Texas, and Florida all require owner-builders to provide sealed engineering wherever the code requires engineering — none grants an owner-builder exemption — but the triggers, the board rules, and the review emphasis differ enough that an owner-builder moving between states needs a fresh briefing each time. Florida sets the most explicit statutory thresholds: the building code and state statutes spell out when a PE seal is required by project type and size, and Florida reviewers apply those thresholds literally — I have seen owner-builders surprised by how much of a \"simple\" project Florida pulls into sealed engineering, particularly once wind-borne-debris and flood provisions enter the picture. Texas works more from the responsible-charge framework: TBPELS Rule 137.33 governs how the PE seals, the firm's F-number goes on the documents, and the AHJ's checklist determines what needs sealing — with expansive-soil foundations and Gulf Coast wind shaping the review substance. Arizona runs through the Board of Technical Registration with jurisdiction-level variation: Phoenix, Tucson, and the counties each apply the state framework through their own submittal cultures, and Valley expansive soils plus monsoon drainage drive the engineering.\n\nI advise owner-builders to learn the new state's triggers before designing, because the design assumptions that avoided engineering in one state trigger it in another. The Texas owner-builder whose slab-on-grade home needed no PE seal discovers Florida wants sealed engineering for the wind design; the Florida owner-builder accustomed to threshold checklists discovers Arizona's jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction variation. The constant across all three: the PE must be licensed in the project state, must perform a genuine review under responsible charge, and seals only what was actually reviewed. No state accepts another state's seal, and no state accepts a seal without the review.\n\nPractically, the comparison comes down to three questions I answer for every relocating owner-builder: what triggers sealed engineering here (thresholds and AHJ checklist), who can seal it (the state board's licensing and the firm's registration requirements), and what will the review emphasize (the local hazards — wind, flood, expansive soil, drainage — that shape the PE's scrutiny). Get those three answers in a pre-design consultation with a locally licensed PE, and the project starts on the right footing in any of the three states. Our /pe-stamp/ practice works with owner-builders across Arizona, Texas, and Florida — one review standard, local licenses, local AHJ fluency.",
